 "notebook": {
  "title": "H₂ σu",
  "subtitle": "",
  "text": "# Hydrogen, H₂ — the antibonding 1σu\n\nTwo protons 1.400 a₀ apart, solved by restricted Hartree–Fock in the minimal STO-3G basis: 2 orbitals, 1 occupied, E = −1.11671 E_h.\n\nThe cloud is orbital 2 of 2, the LUMO, named **1σu**: the two 1s functions taken with opposite signs, σ*(1s) — one lobe on each proton, opposite in phase, with a nodal plane between them. Turn the ORBITAL dial in MOLECULES to 1 for **1σg**, their sum, which gathers the density between the nuclei. ε(1σg) = −0.578 E_h, ε(1σu) = +0.670 E_h.\n\n**These are the H₂⁺ window's two orbitals.** That window was retired in 0.5.0. In a basis of two 1s functions, σg and σu are fixed by symmetry alone, so the combinations it drew for the one-electron ion are the ones on this stage. Only the energies belong to H₂'s two electrons. An older project whose field H₂⁺ owned opens here.\n\nIn MO-REGISTRY, left-click 1σg and 1σu on the ORBITAL ladder, switch MO-REGISTRY ON and press Space: their sum sits on one proton, and the density moves from one proton to the other at the frozen-orbital gap Δε = 1.248 E_h.\n\nUnder the chevron in MOLECULES, set KICK AXIS to Z — along the bond — then KICK and RUN: H₂'s one transition in this basis, 1σg → 1σu, is polarised along the bond, and Im α(ω) grows from it."
 }
